2. THEORETICAL BACKGROUND

We briefly recapitulate the foundational concepts that will be woven together.

2.1 Cognitive State Spaces

Contemporary neuroscience, particularly EEG‑based brain‑computer interfaces, supports the representation of a moment‑to‑moment cognitive state as a point in a low‑dimensional manifold. Spectral power in canonical frequency bands, inter‑electrode coherence, and frontal asymmetry indices can be concatenated into a vector \mathbf{c}(t) \in \mathbb{R}^n sampled at approximately 1 Hz. This yields a very low information rate (≈10²–10³ bits/s) compared to the megabits per second typical of full motor imagery or video. In the CLNS framework, this low bandwidth is not a defect; it is what makes the channel isomorphic to a slowly varying climate variable rather than a control signal.

2.2 Autonomous Agent Populations as Evolving Systems

We model an autonomous artificial civilization as a multi‑agent population \mathcal{P}(t) in which each agent possesses a heritable policy \pi: \mathcal{S} \to \mathcal{A}, parameterised by a vector \mathbf{s} \in \mathcal{G} (the “genotype” space). The population evolves over discrete generations indexed by k. At each generation, agents are evaluated by a fitness function F(\mathbf{s}), and a new population is produced via selection and variation (mutation, recombination). This is the standard substrate of evolutionary computation, but here the fitness function itself is allowed to vary in time, driven by an external, delayed cognitive signal.

2.3 Transfer Entropy and Causal Information Flow

Transfer entropy (Schreiber, 2000) is a model‑free, directed measure of information transfer between time series. For processes X and Y,

T_{X \to Y} = \sum p(y_{t+1}, y_t^{(k)}, x_t^{(l)}) \log \frac{p(y_{t+1} \mid y_t^{(k)}, x_t^{(l)})}{p(y_{t+1} \mid y_t^{(k)})},

where y_t^{(k)} and x_t^{(l)} denote finite histories. This quantity is zero precisely when X contributes no predictive information about Y beyond Y's own past. In the presence of a known delay \Delta, we compute T_{X \to Y}^{(\Delta)} by aligning x_t with y_{t+\Delta}. This will be our primary tool for detecting cognitive selection pressure.

2.4 Fitness Landscapes and the NK Model

Wright’s fitness landscape metaphor assigns a scalar fitness to every point in genotype space. The NK model (Kauffman, 1993) parameterises landscape ruggedness by K, the number of epistatic interactions per gene. High K yields many local optima and favours exploration; low K yields a smooth, single‑peaked landscape amenable to exploitation. We will later use arousal as a modulator of K.

