3. THE CLNS FRAMEWORK: FORMAL DEFINITION

3.1 Core Objects

· Cognitive state vector: \mathbf{c}(t) \in \mathcal{C} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n, sampled at frequency f_s at the human operator site.
· Autonomous civilization: \mathcal{A}(t) = \langle \mathcal{P}(t), F_t(\cdot) \rangle, where \mathcal{P}(t) is the population of agents and F_t is the time‑varying fitness function.
· Communication delay: A fixed one‑way latency \Delta. We assume the round‑trip time is 2\Delta (negligible processing delays).

3.2 The Interaction Loop

The interaction proceeds in discrete epochs of length 2\Delta:

1. At t = 0, the operator receives a report \mathcal{R} describing the state of \mathcal{A} at time -\Delta.
2. The operator’s brain generates a cognitive response \mathbf{c}(0) (a natural, largely involuntary reaction to the report).
3. \mathbf{c}(0) is transmitted and arrives at the civilization at time \Delta.
4. From \Delta to 2\Delta, the civilization evolves under the modified fitness landscape F(\mathbf{s}) = F_0(\mathbf{s}) + \lambda \Phi(\mathbf{c}(0)).
5. A new report is generated based on \mathcal{A}(2\Delta) and sent to the operator, completing the cycle.

This is not a control loop in the classical sense; there is no error correction within a single epoch. It is an evolutionary cycle with an externally modulated fitness function.

3.3 Cognitive Selection Pressure Defined

We define the cognitive selection pressure \Xi(t) as the gradient of the fitness landscape with respect to the agent policy vector, evaluated in the direction most sensitive to the cognitive input:

\Xi(t) = \nabla_{\mathbf{s}} \langle \mathbf{s}, \Phi(\mathbf{c}(t-\Delta)) \rangle,

i.e., the vector of partial derivatives of the fitness perturbation contributed by \mathbf{c}. In practice, we measure \Xi indirectly through the shift it produces in the population distribution over policy space.

