5. INFORMATION‑THEORETIC FOUNDATIONS

5.1 Cognitive Channel Capacity and the Selection‑Drift Bound

The operator transmits one cognitive sample per round‑trip cycle of length 2\Delta. If each transmission contains at most C_{\text{cog}} bits of information, the maximum rate of selective information is

I_{\text{sel}} \le \frac{C_{\text{cog}}}{2\Delta}.

For \Delta \approx 600 s and C_{\text{cog}} \approx 1000 bits, this yields ≈0.83 bit/s. This is vanishingly small compared to control bandwidths, but evolution is a cumulative process. To establish that such a weak signal can overcome random drift, we invoke population genetics: for a selective coefficient s and an effective population size N_e of distinct policy variants, selection reliably drives allele frequency change when N_e s \gg 1. We estimate s induced by a single cognitive update. Let \Delta \bar{f} be the average fitness difference between two policy types caused by the cognitive perturbation. With fitness variance scaling as \lambda^2 \operatorname{Var}(\Phi), the typical selective advantage per generation is s \sim \lambda \sigma_\Phi. The condition for cognitive selection to dominate drift over G generations becomes

G \cdot N_e \cdot \lambda \sigma_\Phi \gg 1.

Given plausible simulation parameters ( N_e \approx 100, \lambda = 0.1, \sigma_\Phi \approx 0.05 ), a few hundred generations suffice. Thus the signal, though slow, is information‑theoretically capable of shaping evolution.

5.2 Transfer Entropy as the Empirical Signature

The fundamental empirical test is whether T_{\mathbf{c} \to \mathcal{A}}^{(\Delta)} > 0 at statistically significant levels, where \mathcal{A} is represented by a low‑dimensional summary statistic of the population (e.g., the mean policy vector \bar{\mathbf{s}}(t)). To rule out spurious correlations due to shared temporal structure, we compare against a surrogate null distribution: the cognitive time series is phase‑randomised or block‑shuffled, destroying its sequential coupling to the civilization while preserving marginal statistics. A statistically significant positive difference

T_{\mathbf{c} \to \mathcal{A}}^{\text{original}} - T_{\mathbf{c} \to \mathcal{A}}^{\text{surrogate}} > 0

confirms that the temporal ordering of cognitive states carries causal information about civilizational change. This is a far more stringent test than simple mutual information.

5.3 Partial Information Decomposition

Using the PID framework (Williams & Beer, 2010), we can decompose the mutual information between the cognitive sequence and the civilizational trajectory into synergistic, redundant, and unique components. The unique information I_{\text{unq}}(\mathbf{c}; \mathcal{A})—the part of the predictive power that \mathbf{c} provides and the civilization’s own past does not—is the formal measure of cognitive climate. The hypothesis is that this quantity exceeds zero by a margin that cannot be explained by finite‑sample effects.

