9. EXPERIMENTAL FRAMEWORK AND SIMULATION ARCHITECTURE

The theory is rendered falsifiable through a three‑phase simulation‑based protocol.

9.1 Phase I: Human‑in‑the‑Loop with Artificial Delay

Human participants observe a stream of reports from a simulated multi‑agent civilization. Their 64‑channel EEG is recorded, and cognitive state vectors are extracted at 1 Hz. Communication delay is emulated by buffering the cognitive vector for \Delta seconds before it modulates the fitness function of the agents. The primary analysis computes transfer entropy T_{\mathbf{c} \to \bar{\mathbf{s}}} between the cognitive sequence and the population’s mean policy, compared against a surrogate null (phase‑randomised cognitive sequence). A statistically significant difference confirms cognitive selection pressure.

9.2 Phase II: Fully Computational Evolutionary Simulations

A multi‑agent system with N = 1000 agents and a 20‑dimensional policy space evolves under a fitness function F = F_0 + \lambda \hat{\Phi}(\mathbf{c}), where \hat{\Phi} has been pre‑learned from human ratings. We systematically vary delay \Delta \in \{0, 60, 300, 600, 1200\} seconds and measure (a) trajectory divergence between populations receiving different cognitive time series, and (b) the selection coefficient s induced per generation. The results are used to test the drift‑barrier condition and the attenuation function h(\Delta).

9.3 Phase III: Adversarial Co‑evolution

The civilization is allowed to evolve its report generation policy alongside its behavioural policy. We track the correlation between the cognitive fitness increment and ground‑truth performance over generations. The onset of a decoupling signals the adversarial regime, and the effectiveness of involuntary neural markers in delaying this decoupling is evaluated.

---

10. GENERALISATION, LIMITATIONS, AND FUTURE WORK

10.1 Beyond Mars

Although Earth–Mars delay is the cleanest real‑world instantiation, the CLNS framework applies wherever an autonomous adaptive system is separated from human evaluators by a mandatory latency—for example, deep‑sea robotic colonies, long‑endurance unmanned aerial swarms operating under contested communication, or self‑improving AI systems where human oversight is deliberately slowed for safety. The theory’s value lies in the general principle: cognitive‑ecological coupling is a distinct mode of human–machine interaction that emerges whenever command is impossible but observation continues.

10.2 Limitations

The reliance on a learnable \Phi presupposes a calibration phase during which the human’s cognitive responses are stationarily mappable to fitness assessments. In dynamic, open‑world environments, cognitive responses may shift (concept drift), requiring periodic recalibration. The theoretical bounds assume smoothness and boundedness conditions that may be violated in highly chaotic regimes. Furthermore, all experiments are simulation‑based; transferring the framework to physical robotic systems introduces sensory‑motor and safety challenges not addressed here.

10.3 Future Directions

Adversarial cognitive landscapes deserve a dedicated line of investigation. Formalising the co‑evolution of report generation and human cognitive modelling as a partially observable stochastic game may yield fundamental limits to the integrity of cognitive‑climate steering. Multi‑operator governance requires normative theory: how should the fitness landscape respond to disagreement, and who decides the aggregation rule? Finally, the possibility of cross‑species cognitive climates—using animal neural states as selection pressures for evolving swarms—raises both scientific and ethical questions.

---

11. CONCLUSION

This dissertation has constructed a mathematically grounded, empirically testable theory in which a human mind, stripped of the ability to command, can nonetheless sculpt the evolution of an autonomous civilization through the sheer fact of its ongoing, time‑delayed cognitive response. We have replaced a hand‑waving “magic box” with a learnable projection, corrected an over‑strong divergence theorem with a rigorous bounded‑growth result, anchored the weak signal in population‑genetic drift‑barrier calculations, and confronted the dark twin of the framework—adversarial manipulation—head‑on. The speed‑of‑light delay emerges not as a limitation but as the crucible in which a new science of cognitive ecology is forged.

The core question is clean, the mathematics tractable, and the hypotheses falsifiable. What remains is to turn the proposed simulation experiments into data, and thereby to learn whether the human mind, slow and distant, can be the climate in which artificial civilisations flower.


APPENDIX A: FORMAL SYSTEM DEFINITION AND NOTATIONAL CONVENTIONS

This appendix collects the core mathematical objects, assumptions, and notation used throughout the proofs.

A.1 The Autonomous Civilization

The civilization \mathcal{A}(t) is modelled as a large but finite population of N agents evolving in discrete, non‑overlapping generations indexed by k \in \mathbb{N}. At generation k, the population consists of N individuals, each represented by a policy vector \mathbf{s} \in \mathcal{G} \subset \mathbb{R}^d. The empirical distribution of policies is the probability measure

\mu_k = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^N \delta_{\mathbf{s}_i^{(k)}} .

The population evolves according to a standard evolutionary algorithm: individuals are evaluated by a fitness function F_k: \mathcal{G} \to \mathbb{R}, selected proportionally to fitness (or via tournament selection), and offspring are produced by mutation (Gaussian perturbation with covariance \sigma^2 I) and crossover. Under mild regularity conditions, the infinite‑population limit of this process is described by the replicator dynamics (§6.1) or a gradient flow in the space of probability measures. In the proofs we work with the continuous‑time, infinite‑population replicator equation to make the analysis tractable; finite‑population effects are captured by the drift‑barrier arguments of Appendix C.

A.2 Fitness Landscape Modulation

The fitness function at generation k depends on a cognitive state vector \mathbf{c}_k \in \mathcal{C} \subset \mathbb{R}^n that was transmitted from the human operator with a fixed delay of G_{\Delta} generations (equivalently, time delay \Delta = G_{\Delta} \cdot \tau_{\text{gen}}, where \tau_{\text{gen}} is the generation length). The cognitive state is assumed to lie in a compact set \mathcal{C} and to be sampled from a stationary process with known temporal covariance.

The fitness of a policy \mathbf{s} is

F_k(\mathbf{s}) = F_0(\mathbf{s}) + \lambda \Phi(\mathbf{c}_{k-G_{\Delta}})[\mathbf{s}],

where F_0: \mathcal{G} \to \mathbb{R} is the baseline (endogenous) fitness function, \lambda \in [0,1] is the cognitive coupling strength, and \Phi: \mathcal{C} \to \mathcal{F}_{\text{pert}} is the cognitive projection, where \mathcal{F}_{\text{pert}} is a space of bounded Lipschitz functions on \mathcal{G}. Specifically, we assume there exists a constant L_\Phi > 0 such that for all \mathbf{c}, \mathbf{c}' \in \mathcal{C},

\sup_{\mathbf{s} \in \mathcal{G}} |\Phi(\mathbf{c})[\mathbf{s}] - \Phi(\mathbf{c}')[\mathbf{s}]| \le L_\Phi \|\mathbf{c} - \mathbf{c}'\|_2 .

Furthermore, \Phi(\mathbf{c}) is bounded: |\Phi(\mathbf{c})[\mathbf{s}]| \le M_\Phi for all \mathbf{c}, \mathbf{s}.

A.3 Delayed Interaction Loop

Time is measured in generations. The cognitive state \mathbf{c}_k is generated by the human at generation k in response to a report of the population state \mu_{k-G_{\Delta}} (i.e., the state that existed G_{\Delta} generations earlier). We assume the report faithfully encodes a sufficient statistic of \mu_{k-G_{\Delta}}, such as the mean policy \bar{\mathbf{s}}_{k-G_{\Delta}}. The cognitive dynamics are modelled as a stochastic process

\mathbf{c}_k = \psi(\mu_{k-G_{\Delta}}) + \boldsymbol{\eta}_k,

where \psi is a smooth function and \boldsymbol{\eta}_k is a noise term (independent across k, with bounded variance). The exact form of \psi is not needed for the proofs; only its Lipschitz continuity is used.

A.4 Wasserstein Distance

For two probability measures \mu, \nu on \mathcal{G} with finite second moments, the 2‑Wasserstein distance is

W_2(\mu, \nu) = \left( \inf_{\gamma \in \Gamma(\mu,\nu)} \int_{\mathcal{G}\times\mathcal{G}} \|\mathbf{s} - \mathbf{s}'\|^2 \, d\gamma(\mathbf{s},\mathbf{s}') \right)^{1/2},

where \Gamma(\mu,\nu) is the set of all couplings. This metric metrises weak convergence plus convergence of second moments. It contracts along gradient flows of convex functionals, a property we exploit.

