APPENDIX C: INFORMATION‑THEORETIC DETAILS AND THE SELECTION‑DRIFT BARRIER

C.1 Transfer Entropy Estimation and Surrogate Null

For two stationary time series \{X_t\}, \{Y_t\} with a known delay \Delta, we estimate transfer entropy T_{X \to Y}^{(\Delta)} using a kernel‑based estimator (Schreiber, 2000). The null distribution is obtained by phase randomisation of the source series: we compute the Fourier transform of \{X_t\}, randomise the phases while preserving the power spectrum, and invert to obtain a surrogate series \{X_t^{\text{surr}}\} that has exactly the same autocorrelation structure but no phase‑locked coupling to \{Y_t\}. The test statistic is

\Delta T = \hat{T}_{X \to Y}^{(\Delta)} - \frac{1}{B} \sum_{b=1}^B \hat{T}_{X^{\text{surr},b} \to Y}^{(\Delta)},

and significance is assessed via rank ordering.

C.2 Drift Barrier and Minimum Effective Signal

In a finite population of N individuals with n_e effectively neutral dimensions, genetic drift causes stochastic fluctuations of order 1/\sqrt{N} per generation. A selective advantage s between two alleles (policies) results in a deterministic frequency change \approx s x(1-x). The condition for selection to dominate drift is N_e s \gg 1.

We derive s from the cognitive perturbation. Let two policies \mathbf{s}_1, \mathbf{s}_2 differ only in the dimension most sensitive to the cognitive input. Their fitness difference is

\Delta F = \lambda \left( \Phi(\mathbf{c})[\mathbf{s}_1] - \Phi(\mathbf{c})[\mathbf{s}_2] \right).

Averaging over the cognitive state distribution, the expected selective advantage is s = \lambda \sigma_\Phi \cdot d_{\text{sens}}, where \sigma_\Phi is the standard deviation of the projection difference and d_{\text{sens}} encodes the alignment. Taking d_{\text{sens}} = 1 for the maximally sensitive direction, the condition for effective selection after G generations is

G \, N_e \, \lambda \, \sigma_\Phi \gg 1.

Plugging in plausible simulation values (N_e \approx 100, \lambda = 0.1, \sigma_\Phi = 0.05, G = 200) gives G N_e \lambda \sigma_\Phi = 100, easily satisfying the condition. Thus, even with the minuscule information rate, the cumulative effect is detectable over reasonable experimental timescales.

