APPENDIX G: PARTIAL INFORMATION DECOMPOSITION AND UNIQUE INFORMATION

The mutual information between the cognitive sequence \mathbf{C}_{1:T} = \{\mathbf{c}_1, \dots, \mathbf{c}_T\} and the civilizational trajectory \mathbf{A}_{1:T} can be decomposed into synergistic, redundant, and unique components relative to the civilization’s own past \mathbf{A}_{1:T}^{-} (a time‑lagged embedding). The unique information I_{\text{unq}}(\mathbf{C}; \mathbf{A} \mid \mathbf{A}^{-}) is the part of the mutual information that can only be obtained from the cognitive state, not from the civilization’s own history. We estimate it using the Williams–Beer (2010) redundancy lattice, specifically the I_{\min} measure. Under the null hypothesis of no cognitive climate, I_{\text{unq}} = 0. A non‑zero unique information, statistically validated against surrogates, provides direct evidence for the human‑as‑climate mechanism.

---

APPENDIX H: CRITICAL DELAY THRESHOLD DERIVATION

The fitness perturbation received at the civilization side at time t is \lambda \Phi(\mathbf{c}(t-\Delta)). The effective signal strength decays with \Delta due to the attenuation function h(\Delta). Incorporating this into the drift‑barrier condition, the effective selection coefficient becomes s(\Delta) = \lambda \sigma_\Phi h(\Delta). The critical delay \Delta_{\text{crit}} is the value for which G N_e s(\Delta) \approx 1. Solving G N_e \lambda \sigma_\Phi h(\Delta_{\text{crit}}) = 1 with h(\Delta) = e^{-\gamma \Delta} + \beta yields

\Delta_{\text{crit}} = \frac{1}{\gamma} \log\left( \frac{G N_e \lambda \sigma_\Phi}{1 - G N_e \lambda \sigma_\Phi \beta} \right),

provided the denominator is positive. With realistic neural timescales (\gamma^{-1} \sim 900 s) and \beta = 0.05, the threshold lies comfortably beyond maximum Earth–Mars delays (~1320 s one‑way), confirming the feasibility.

---

APPENDIX I: SIMULATION ARCHITECTURE AND NULL MODEL CONSTRUCTION

The simulation environment is a discrete‑time multi‑agent system with N=1000 agents, each possessing a policy vector \mathbf{s} \in \mathbb{R}^{20}. The baseline fitness is F_0(\mathbf{s}) = \mathbf{s}^\top Q \mathbf{s} + \mathbf{b}^\top \mathbf{s} with Q \prec 0 (ensuring a single peak). The cognitive perturbation \Phi(\mathbf{c})[\mathbf{s}] is implemented as a linear function \mathbf{w}_c^\top \mathbf{s} where \mathbf{w}_c is output by a pre‑trained neural network from the cognitive vector. Reports are generated as summary statistics (mean, variance) of the current population. The null model for transfer entropy testing is constructed by applying an iterative amplitude‑adjusted Fourier transform (IAAFT) to the cognitive time series, preserving both the marginal distribution and the power spectrum while destroying any phase coupling to the civilization. A permutation test with B=500 surrogates yields a p‑value for the one‑sided test T_{\text{orig}} > T_{\text{surr}}.

